
  <sn><md>### Soil Acidification

**Definition:**
Soil acidification is the process by which soil pH decreases (becomes more acidic) due to the accumulation of **H⁺ and Al³⁺ ions** on exchange sites, lowering pH below **7.0** (strongly acidic below 5.5).

**Causes:**
- **Acid rain** — H₂SO₄ and HNO₃ from pollution
- **Fertilizer use** — ammonium fertilizers (NH₄⁺) release H⁺ during nitrification:
$$
\text{NH}_4^+ + 2\text{O}_2 \rightarrow \text{NO}_3^- + 2\text{H}^+ + \text{H}_2\text{O}
$$
- **Organic matter decomposition** — releases organic acids and CO₂
- **Leaching of bases** — Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, K⁺ washed away by rain, leaving H⁺ behind
- **Plant uptake of cations** — roots release H⁺ in exchange for nutrients
- **Mining and industrial activities** — acid mine drainage

**Process:**
$$
\text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + 2\text{H}^+ \rightarrow \text{Soil} - 2\text{H}^+ + \text{Ca}^{2+}
$$
H⁺ displaces base cations from exchange sites. As pH drops, **Al³⁺** becomes soluble and replaces more bases.

**Effects on Soil:**
- **Nutrient deficiency** — Ca, Mg, K, P become unavailable
- **Al³⁺ and Mn²⁺ toxicity** — damage plant roots
- **Reduced microbial activity** — nitrogen fixation and decomposition slow down
- **Poor nutrient cycling** — organic matter breakdown decreases
- **Leaching of nutrients** — bases lost from root zone

**Effects on Crops:**
- Stunted root growth (Al toxicity)
- Poor nutrient uptake
- Yellowing and stunted plants
- Reduced crop yield

**Remediation:**
- **Liming** — adding CaCO₃ or Ca(OH)₂:
$$
\text{Soil} - 2\text{H}^+ + \text{CaCO}_3 \rightarrow \text{Soil} - \text{Ca}^{2+} + \text{H}_2\text{O} + \text{CO}_2
$$
- **Organic matter addition** — buffers pH
- **Balanced fertilizer use** — avoid excess ammonium fertilizers
- **Growing acid-tolerant crops** — tea, potato, pineapple

**Example:**
In **tea-growing regions of Assam (India)**, continuous use of ammonium fertilizers and heavy rainfall has acidified soils (pH &lt; 5.0), requiring regular liming.

**Conclusion:**
Soil acidification is the lowering of soil pH due to H⁺ and Al³⁺ accumulation, causing nutrient deficiency and Al toxicity. It is managed by **liming, organic matter addition, and balanced fertilization**.</md></sn>
